; pkg.ax - `axiom.pkg`, the project manifest.
;
; WHAT A DEPENDENCY WAS BEFORE THIS. A directory on `$AXIOM_PATH`, a
; colon-separated environment variable. That is a real mechanism and it
; stays - but nothing records what a program depends on, so nothing
; travels with the project, nothing can be checked, and two directories
; providing a module of the same name resolve first-wins by environment
; order, silently, with the loser's declarations compiled against
; nobody. It is the value-namespace twin of the type collision `AX3044`
; closed on 2026-08-24, and it had the same failure mode: a wrong
; answer at exit 0.
;
; THE MANIFEST. `axiom.pkg`, at or above the entry file's directory, is
; a line-based file - `#` comments, blank lines ignored, one
; `key value` pair per line:
;
;     # axiom.pkg
;     name     myapp
;     version  0.1.0
;     depend   vendor/axiom-json
;     depend   ../shared/modules
;
; `depend` names a DIRECTORY of modules, resolved against the
; manifest's own directory so the project relocates, and each one joins
; the module search path after the entry file's directory and before
; `$AXIOM_PATH`. `version` is recorded and read by nothing yet; it is
; here because a manifest that cannot say what package it describes is
; a search path with extra steps, and because it is what a registry
; would key on if there is ever one.
;
; `name` IS READ, since 2026-08-31: it is the file `axiom build` writes
; when neither `--output` nor `-o` says otherwise, in place of the
; literal `output` every project shared before. A manifest key that
; nothing reads is a key nobody can tell from a typo, which is the
; other half of this file's job - see below.
;
; A MANIFEST LINE THAT MEANS NOTHING IS REFUSED. Three shapes used to
; be read as silence, measured on the 0.6.1 binary before this changed:
;
;     $ printf 'name\tprobe\ndepend\tvendor/lib\n' > axiom.pkg
;     $ axiom check app.ax
;     error[AX5001]: cannot resolve import `Widget`      # exit 1
;
; a TAB between key and value - the split was `strStartsWith t "depend "`,
; one literal space - and the same wall of missing modules for a
; misspelled key (`dependd vendor/lib`) and for a key with no value at
; all (`depend` alone on its line). In every case the manifest that
; caused it was never named. `pkgCheckLines` now walks the file once
; and refuses an unknown key, a key with no value, a second `name` or
; `version`, and a `name` that is not a file name - each at
; `axiom.pkg:LINE`, before a byte is compiled - and the key/value split
; is any run of whitespace rather than one space.
;
; `crate` NAMES A NATIVE DEPENDENCY, since 2026-09-03. `depend` names
; a directory of `.ax` and nothing else, so a package whose whole
; reason to exist is a Rust archive could not be declared: the shell
; could say it - `AXIOM_PATH=vendor/greeter/axiom axiom build app.ax`
; linked and ran - and the manifest could not. Measured on 0.7.3, one
; source tree, one `libaxiom_greeter.a` already on disk, two ways of
; declaring it: through `$AXIOM_PATH`, exit 0 and the program's answer;
; through `depend vendor/greeter/axiom`, exit 4 with
; `error[AX4004]: no archive is linked`. That is the exact inversion of
; what a manifest is for - the environment could describe the project
; and the project could not.
;
;     crate    vendor/axiom-greeter
;
; names a CRATE DIRECTORY: a `Cargo.toml` beside an `axiom/` holding
; the generated binding module. It joins the module search path in
; `depend`'s slot, as `DIR/axiom/`, and its `target/release` - and its
; workspace's, one and two levels up - joins the link search path.
;
; A MANIFEST `crate` NEVER RUNS CARGO, and that is the decision this
; key is built around rather than an omission. `--crate DIR` on the
; command line does: measured on 0.7.3 against a crate with no
; `target/`, `axiom build app.ax --crate mycrate` printed
; `axiom: building crate `axiom-greeter` with cargo (no archive
; found)`, cargo ran, and `mycrate/target/` appeared. A COMMAND LINE
; is a person asking; a manifest is a checked-in file that arrives with
; a clone. `docs/reference.md` says the compiler executes no code from
; a source file, and a key that let a cloned repository spawn another
; project's build system would make that sentence false for the one
; file whose whole job is to be trusted. So an unbuilt archive stays
; `AX4004`, whose help already names `--link-lib`, `--link-search` and
; the `target/release` it looked in; the user runs `cargo build
; --release` (or one `axiom build --crate DIR`) once, and the manifest
; is enough from then on.
;
; WHAT THIS IS NOT, said out loud so nobody has to discover it: there
; is no registry, no lockfile, no version constraint and no fetching.
; A dependency is a path on this machine - a `crate` too. Every one of
; those is a policy decision that wants a maintainer to make it, and
; shipping a half-made one is worse than shipping the mechanism it
; would rest on. A lockfile in particular is not a step toward a
; fetcher: with every dependency a path the user already controls, a
; digest over it closes no surface and changes on every edit of their
; own vendored code. It becomes meaningful when code starts arriving
; from elsewhere, and not before.
;
; TWO DEPENDENCIES MAY NOT PROVIDE ONE MODULE. That is the property
; that makes this more than `$AXIOM_PATH`, and `pkgVerify` refuses it
; before a byte is compiled, naming both files and the manifest. It is
; checked at the MANIFEST rather than per-import on purpose: the fault
; is in the project's configuration, and reporting it only for the
; imports that happen to be ambiguous would let a program build today
; and stop building when somebody adds an import.
;
; ...AND THE PROPERTY WAS TOP-LEVEL ONLY until 2026-09-03. `depend a`
; and `depend b` each holding `Sub/Widget.ax` - the file
; `(import Sub.Widget)` resolves to - built and exited with `a`'s
; answer, silently, because `pkgFirstShared` did one `sysReadDir` and
; never descended. The gate could not see it either: its fixture
; helper only ever wrote top-level modules. `pkgModules` now walks a
; dependency three levels deep, the same depth the resolver's own
; nested lookup is documented at, and the refusal names the module by
; the dotted name an `import` would spell.
;
; A DEPENDENCY MAY NOT PROVIDE A STANDARD-LIBRARY MODULE. Same fault,
; same silence, one directory further down the ladder: `depend` sits
; ABOVE the standard library in the search order, so a dependency
; carrying a `Fmt.ax` replaced the real one for the whole program.
; Measured on 0.7.3 - a vendored copy of `stdlib/Fmt.ax` with `fmtInt`
; rewritten to answer `"HIJACKED"` made the program print HIJACKED at
; exit 0, no diagnostic. That is a supply-chain surface wearing a
; search path, and it is refused now. The refusal lives in
; `codegen.ax`, beside the search order it is about, because this file
; cannot see one: `codegen` imports `pkg`, not the other way round.
; The ENTRY FILE'S own directory is untouched and still shadows a
; library module, because that is documented, intended, and is a file
; the author wrote rather than one that arrived with a dependency.
(import Str)
(import Vec)
(import Sys)
(import IO (fileExists isDir readFile writeStr))
(import Path)
(import Fmt)

; The manifest's file name. One place, because three functions here
; and one message all say it.
(pub :: pkgManifestName String)
(pub fn (pkgManifestName)
  "axiom.pkg")

; The nearest `axiom.pkg` at or above `dir`, or "".
;
; UP TO EIGHT LEVELS. A bound rather than a walk to the root, because
; an unbounded walk reads a file per level of every path anyone ever
; compiles from, and because a manifest eight directories above the
; file being compiled is more likely to be somebody else's than yours.
; The number is stated here rather than derived: no project in this
; repository is deeper than three.
(pub :: pkgFindManifest (-> String String))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgFindManifest dir)
  (pkgFindManifestFrom
    (if (== (strLen dir) 0)
      "./"
      (pathWithSlash dir))
    0))

(pub :: pkgFindManifestFrom (-> String Int String))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgFindManifestFrom dir depth)
  (if (> depth 8)
    ""
    (let ((cand (concat dir pkgManifestName)))
      (if (fileExists cand)
        cand
        (let ((up (pathWithSlash (concat dir ".."))))
          (pkgFindManifestFrom up (+ depth 1)))))))

; The value of the first `key` line in `text`, or "".
(pub :: pkgValue (-> String String String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgValue text key)
  (let (
    (lines (strSplit text 10))
    (n (vecLen lines))
    (mut i 0)
  )
    (let ((mut found ""))
      {
        (while (< i n)
          {
            ; Both arms answer `Int`: `set` answers the value it
            ; assigned, which is a `String` here, and an `if` whose
            ; arms disagree is a type error reported against the `0`.
            (let ((v (pkgLineValue (vecGetStr lines i) key)))
              (if (&& (== (strLen found) 0) (> (strLen v) 0))
                {
                  (set found v)
                  0
                }
                0))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        found
      })))

; Every value of `key`, in file order.
(pub :: pkgValues (-> String String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgValues text key)
  (let (
    (lines (strSplit text 10))
    (out vecNew)
    (n (vecLen lines))
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (let ((v (pkgLineValue (vecGetStr lines i) key)))
            (if (> (strLen v) 0)
              (vecPush
                out
                (cast Int v))
              (cast (Vec Int) 0)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      out
    }))

; One line's value for `key`, or "".
;
; The key must be the line's WHOLE first token, so `dependency` is not
; read as `depend`, and a `#` anywhere starts a comment - which means a
; path may not contain one. That is a restriction worth stating rather
; than a bug: a `#` in a path is legal on every filesystem here, and a
; manifest format with no way to write a comment is a manifest format
; nobody annotates.
;
; THE TOKEN IS TAKEN, NOT MATCHED. This used to ask
; `strStartsWith t (concat key " ")` - the key followed by one
; literal space - which read `depend<TAB>vendor/lib` as no line at all
; and reported it as every module the directory would have provided
; going missing. `pkgKeyOf` splits on any whitespace, so a tab-aligned
; manifest means what it looks like, and the token it returns is what
; `pkgCheckLines` refuses when it is not a key.
(pub :: pkgLineValue (-> String String String))
(pub fn (pkgLineValue line key)
  (let ((t (pkgTrim (pkgUncomment line))))
    (let ((k (pkgKeyOf t)))
      (if (strEq k key)
        (pkgTrim (strSlice t (strLen k) (- (strLen t) (strLen k))))
        ""))))

; The leading run of non-space bytes of an already-trimmed line: its
; key, or "" for a line that is blank once its comment is removed.
(pub :: pkgKeyOf (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgKeyOf t)
  (let (
    (n (strLen t))
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (if (< i n)
        (if (strIsSpace (strByte t i))
          false
          true)
        false)
        (set i (+ i 1)))
      (strSlice t 0 i)
    }))

; The six keys a manifest may write. A closed set on purpose: the
; format has no version field of its own, so a key it does not know is
; a typo rather than a message from the future. `opt` is the project's
; default optimisation level and `main` its entry file; both are read
; where the command line is silent, and both are refused here when they
; cannot mean what they say.
(pub :: pkgKnownKey (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (pkgKnownKey k)
  (|| (strEq k "name") (|| (strEq k "version") (|| (strEq k "depend") (|| (strEq k "crate") (|| (strEq k "opt") (strEq k "main")))))))

(pub :: pkgUncomment (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgUncomment line)
  (match (strFindByte line 35 0)
    ((None) line)
    ((Some at) (strSlice line 0 at))))

(pub :: pkgTrim (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgTrim s)
  (let (
    (n (strLen s))
    (mut a 0)
    (mut b (strLen s))
  )
    {
      (while (if (< a b)
        (strIsSpace (strByte s a))
        false)
        (set a (+ a 1)))
      (while (if (< a b)
        (strIsSpace (strByte s (- b 1)))
        false)
        (set b (- b 1)))
      (strSlice s a (- b a))
    }))

; The dependency directories the manifest at or above `entryDir`
; declares, each with a trailing slash and resolved against the
; MANIFEST's directory - so `depend vendor/lib` means the same thing
; wherever the project is checked out and whatever directory the
; compiler is run from.
;
; An empty Vec when there is no manifest, which is every invocation
; this repository's own gates make: the mechanism costs a project that
; does not use it one `sysFileExists` per directory level.
(pub :: pkgDependDirs (-> String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgDependDirs entryDir)
  (pkgDirsFor entryDir "depend"))

; The CRATE directories the manifest declares, same shape and same
; resolution rule. A crate directory is not a directory of modules: it
; holds a `Cargo.toml` and an `axiom/` with the generated binding
; module in it, so `pkgCrateAxiomDirs` is what joins the module search
; path and the directory itself is what the link search is built from.
;
; Nothing here spawns cargo, and `pkgVerify` refuses a crate whose
; `axiom/` is not there rather than generating one - see this file's
; header for why a checked-in line may not run another project's build
; system.
(pub :: pkgCrateDirs (-> String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgCrateDirs entryDir)
  (pkgDirsFor entryDir "crate"))

; `DIR/axiom/` for each declared crate, in file order: the one
; directory of a crate that holds `.ax`.
(pub :: pkgCrateAxiomDirs (-> String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgCrateAxiomDirs entryDir)
  (pkgAxiomSub (pkgCrateDirs entryDir)))

; The shared body of the two above: every value of `key`, resolved
; against the manifest's directory, each with a trailing slash.
(pub :: pkgDirsFor (-> String String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgDirsFor entryDir key)
  (let ((mf (pkgFindManifest entryDir)))
    (if (== (strLen mf) 0)
      vecNew
      (pkgDirsIn (readFile mf) (pathDir mf) key))))

; Every value of `key` in an ALREADY-READ manifest, resolved against
; `base`. Split from the function above so that `pkgSearchDirs` can ask
; for two keys over one read - see its comment for why that matters.
;
; A `depend` value that is a git URL resolves to its deterministic
; checkout (`.axiom/deps/<slug>/`) rather than against `base` - the
; URL is where the dependency lives, the checkout is where it is
; read from, and `pkgVerify` refuses the latter with the clone that
; fills it when it is not there.
(pub :: pkgDirsIn (-> String String String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgDirsIn text base key)
  (let ((raw (pkgValues text key)))
    (let (
      (out vecNew)
      (n (vecLen raw))
      (mut i 0)
    )
      {
        (while (< i n)
          {
            (vecPush
              out
              (cast Int (pkgResolveDep text base key (vecGetStr raw i))))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        out
      })))

(pub :: pkgResolveDep (-> String String String String String))
(pub fn (pkgResolveDep text base key v)
  (if (&& (strEq key "depend") (pkgIsUrl v))
    (pkgDepCheckout base v)
    (pathWithSlash (pkgResolve base v))))

; `DIR/axiom/` for each directory in `dirs`.
(pub :: pkgAxiomSub (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgAxiomSub dirs)
  (let (
    (out vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen dirs))
        {
          (vecPush
            out
            (cast Int (concat (vecGetStr dirs i) "axiom/")))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      out
    }))

; EVERY module search directory the manifest contributes, in order:
; each `depend`, then each `crate`'s `axiom/`.
;
; ONE manifest walk and ONE read for both keys, and that is the whole
; reason this exists rather than `moduleSearchDirs` calling
; `pkgDependDirs` and `pkgCrateAxiomDirs` in turn. `moduleSearchDirs`
; runs PER MODULE LOOKUP, and each of those two would repeat
; `pkgFindManifest`'s walk - up to eight `sysFileExists` on a tree with
; no manifest at all - so the obvious spelling would have doubled a
; cost this file bounds rather than pays. Measured after the fact is
; too late for a cost that is paid by every project in the world that
; does not use the feature.
(pub :: pkgSearchDirs (-> String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgSearchDirs entryDir)
  (let ((mf (pkgFindManifest entryDir)))
    (if (== (strLen mf) 0)
      vecNew
      (let (
        (text (readFile mf))
        (base (pathDir mf))
      )
        (pkgConcatDirs (pkgDirsIn text base "depend") (pkgAxiomSub (pkgDirsIn text base "crate")))))))

; A manifest path against the manifest's own directory. An absolute
; path is taken as it stands.
(pub :: pkgResolve (-> String String String))
(pub fn (pkgResolve base p)
  (if (pathIsAbsolute p)
    p
    (concat (pathWithSlash base) p)))

; A `depend` value that names a registry location rather than a
; directory: for now, a git URL. The registry is a URL on purpose -
; there is no index to publish to and no lockfile to resolve, so the
; manifest says exactly where its dependency lives, byte for byte.
; `file://` names a local repository and exists so the fetch is
; testable with no network: a gate builds a repository in a scratch
; directory and depends on it through its own path.
(pub :: pkgIsUrl (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (pkgIsUrl v)
  (|| (strStartsWith v "https://") (|| (strStartsWith v "http://") (|| (strStartsWith v "git@") (|| (strStartsWith v "ssh://") (|| (strStartsWith v "git://") (strStartsWith v "file://")))))))

; A URL's deterministic checkout segment: the scheme gone, one
; trailing `/` gone, one trailing `.git` gone, and every byte outside
; `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` a `-`. Flat by construction - no `/` survives, so
; the segment cannot escape the directory that holds it - and stable,
; so two manifests naming one URL name one checkout.
(pub :: pkgDepSlug (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgDepSlug url)
  (pkgSlugBytes (pkgStripDotGit (pkgStripSlash (pkgStripScheme url))) 0))

(pub :: pkgStripScheme (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgStripScheme url)
  (if (strStartsWith url "https://")
    (strSlice url 8 (- (strLen url) 8))
    (if (strStartsWith url "http://")
      (strSlice url 7 (- (strLen url) 7))
      (if (strStartsWith url "git@")
        (strSlice url 4 (- (strLen url) 4))
        (if (strStartsWith url "ssh://")
          (strSlice url 6 (- (strLen url) 6))
          (if (strStartsWith url "git://")
            (strSlice url 6 (- (strLen url) 6))
            (if (strStartsWith url "file://")
              (strSlice url 7 (- (strLen url) 7))
              url)))))))

(pub :: pkgStripSlash (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgStripSlash s)
  (if (&& (> (strLen s) 0) (== (strByte s (- (strLen s) 1)) 47))
    (strSlice s 0 (- (strLen s) 1))
    s))

(pub :: pkgStripDotGit (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgStripDotGit s)
  (if (&& (> (strLen s) 4) (strEq (strSlice s (- (strLen s) 4) 4) ".git"))
    (strSlice s 0 (- (strLen s) 4))
    s))

(pub :: pkgSlugBytes (-> String Int String))
(pub fn (pkgSlugBytes s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    ""
    (let ((b (strByte s i)))
      (concat
        (if (pkgIsSlugByte b)
          (strSlice s i 1)
          "-")
        (pkgSlugBytes s (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: pkgIsSlugByte (-> Int Bool))
(pub fn (pkgIsSlugByte b)
  (|| (strIsAlpha b) (|| (strIsDigit b) (|| (== b 46) (|| (== b 45) (== b 95))))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The checkout KEY, and the hash inside it.
;
; `pkgDepSlug` alone was the checkout directory until 2026-09-26, and
; it is not injective: every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` becomes `-`,
; so `file:///repos/a/b` and `file:///repos/a-b` both named
; `.axiom/deps/repos-a-b/`. An audit fetched the first (its `Widget.answer`
; is 11), pointed the manifest at the second (22), and `axiom fetch`
; printed `present` and exited 0 - the program still answered 11,
; compiled against a repository the manifest no longer named. Changing a
; dependency silently kept the old one.
;
; So the key is the slug, cut to 64 bytes for a readable prefix, then a
; `-`, then the first 32 hex digits (128 bits) of the SHA-256 of the
; WHOLE URL exactly as the manifest spells it: two spellings are two
; checkouts, and two different URLs meet in one directory only by a
; collision in SHA-256. The hash is not the only guard - `pkgOrigin`
; below reads the origin git recorded, and a checkout cloned from
; anywhere else is refused whatever its directory is called - but it is
; what lets two dependencies that the slug confused coexist, rather
; than take turns being refused.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: pkgDepKey (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgDepKey url)
  (let ((slug (pkgDepSlug url)))
    (concat
      (if (> (strLen slug) 64)
        (strSlice slug 0 64)
        slug)
      (concat "-" (strSlice (pkgSha256Hex url) 0 32)))))

; SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4), for the key above and nothing else. A 32-bit
; word lives in a non-negative Int, so `>>` is a logical shift here
; (the sign bit is never set) and every sum is masked back to 32 bits.
; `scripts/check-driver.sh` holds a checkout's name to the digest
; `shasum` computes for the same URL, which is what pins this
; implementation: a wrong constant or rotation would name a different
; directory there.
(:: pkgShaMask Int)
(fn (pkgShaMask)
  4294967295)

(:: pkgShaRotr (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (pkgShaRotr x n)
  (& (| (>> x n) (<< x (- 32 n))) pkgShaMask))

; The 64 round constants: the first 32 bits of the fractional parts of
; the cube roots of the first 64 primes.
(:: pkgShaK (Vec Int))
(fn (pkgShaK)
  (let ((k vecNew))
    {
      (vecPush k 1116352408)
      (vecPush k 1899447441)
      (vecPush k 3049323471)
      (vecPush k 3921009573)
      (vecPush k 961987163)
      (vecPush k 1508970993)
      (vecPush k 2453635748)
      (vecPush k 2870763221)
      (vecPush k 3624381080)
      (vecPush k 310598401)
      (vecPush k 607225278)
      (vecPush k 1426881987)
      (vecPush k 1925078388)
      (vecPush k 2162078206)
      (vecPush k 2614888103)
      (vecPush k 3248222580)
      (vecPush k 3835390401)
      (vecPush k 4022224774)
      (vecPush k 264347078)
      (vecPush k 604807628)
      (vecPush k 770255983)
      (vecPush k 1249150122)
      (vecPush k 1555081692)
      (vecPush k 1996064986)
      (vecPush k 2554220882)
      (vecPush k 2821834349)
      (vecPush k 2952996808)
      (vecPush k 3210313671)
      (vecPush k 3336571891)
      (vecPush k 3584528711)
      (vecPush k 113926993)
      (vecPush k 338241895)
      (vecPush k 666307205)
      (vecPush k 773529912)
      (vecPush k 1294757372)
      (vecPush k 1396182291)
      (vecPush k 1695183700)
      (vecPush k 1986661051)
      (vecPush k 2177026350)
      (vecPush k 2456956037)
      (vecPush k 2730485921)
      (vecPush k 2820302411)
      (vecPush k 3259730800)
      (vecPush k 3345764771)
      (vecPush k 3516065817)
      (vecPush k 3600352804)
      (vecPush k 4094571909)
      (vecPush k 275423344)
      (vecPush k 430227734)
      (vecPush k 506948616)
      (vecPush k 659060556)
      (vecPush k 883997877)
      (vecPush k 958139571)
      (vecPush k 1322822218)
      (vecPush k 1537002063)
      (vecPush k 1747873779)
      (vecPush k 1955562222)
      (vecPush k 2024104815)
      (vecPush k 2227730452)
      (vecPush k 2361852424)
      (vecPush k 2428436474)
      (vecPush k 2756734187)
      (vecPush k 3204031479)
      (vecPush k 3329325298)
      k
    }))

; The message's bytes, padded: a 1 bit, zeros to 56 bytes mod 64, then
; the length in bits as eight big-endian bytes.
(:: pkgShaPad (-> String (Vec Int)))
(fn (pkgShaPad s)
  (let (
    (n (strLen s))
    (out vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (vecPush out (strByte s i))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (vecPush out 128)
      (while (!= (% (vecLen out) 64) 56)
        {
          (vecPush out 0)
          0
        })
      (set i 7)
      (while (>= i 0)
        {
          (vecPush out (& (>> (* n 8) (* i 8)) 255))
          (set i (- i 1))
        })
      out
    }))

; One 64-byte block of `m` from `at`, folded into the state `h`; `w` is
; the 64-word schedule, reused across blocks.
(:: pkgShaBlock (-> (Vec Int) Int (Vec Int) (Vec Int) (Vec Int) Int))
(fn (pkgShaBlock m at k h w)
  (let ((mut t 0))
    {
      (while (< t 16)
        {
          (vecSet w t (| (<< (vecGet m (+ at (* t 4))) 24) (| (<< (vecGet m (+ at (+ (* t 4) 1))) 16) (| (<< (vecGet m (+ at (+ (* t 4) 2))) 8) (vecGet m (+ at (+ (* t 4) 3)))))))
          (set t (+ t 1))
        })
      (while (< t 64)
        (let (
          (x (vecGet w (- t 15)))
          (y (vecGet w (- t 2)))
        )
          (let (
            (ls0 (^ (pkgShaRotr x 7) (^ (pkgShaRotr x 18) (>> x 3))))
            (ls1 (^ (pkgShaRotr y 17) (^ (pkgShaRotr y 19) (>> y 10))))
          )
            {
              (vecSet w t (& (+ (+ (vecGet w (- t 16)) ls0) (+ (vecGet w (- t 7)) ls1)) pkgShaMask))
              (set t (+ t 1))
            })))
      (let (
        (mut a (vecGet h 0))
        (mut b (vecGet h 1))
        (mut c (vecGet h 2))
        (mut d (vecGet h 3))
        (mut e (vecGet h 4))
        (mut f (vecGet h 5))
        (mut g (vecGet h 6))
        (mut hh (vecGet h 7))
        (mut i 0)
      )
        {
          (while (< i 64)
            (let (
              (bs1 (^ (pkgShaRotr e 6) (^ (pkgShaRotr e 11) (pkgShaRotr e 25))))
              (ch (^ (& e f) (& (^ e pkgShaMask) g)))
              (bs0 (^ (pkgShaRotr a 2) (^ (pkgShaRotr a 13) (pkgShaRotr a 22))))
              (mj (^ (& a b) (^ (& a c) (& b c))))
            )
              (let (
                (t1 (& (+ (+ hh bs1) (+ ch (+ (vecGet k i) (vecGet w i)))) pkgShaMask))
                (t2 (& (+ bs0 mj) pkgShaMask))
              )
                {
                  (set hh g)
                  (set g f)
                  (set f e)
                  (set e (& (+ d t1) pkgShaMask))
                  (set d c)
                  (set c b)
                  (set b a)
                  (set a (& (+ t1 t2) pkgShaMask))
                  (set i (+ i 1))
                })))
          (vecSet h 0 (& (+ (vecGet h 0) a) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 1 (& (+ (vecGet h 1) b) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 2 (& (+ (vecGet h 2) c) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 3 (& (+ (vecGet h 3) d) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 4 (& (+ (vecGet h 4) e) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 5 (& (+ (vecGet h 5) f) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 6 (& (+ (vecGet h 6) g) pkgShaMask))
          (vecSet h 7 (& (+ (vecGet h 7) hh) pkgShaMask))
          0
        })
    }))

; The SHA-256 of `s`'s bytes, as 64 lower-case hex digits.
(pub :: pkgSha256Hex (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgSha256Hex s)
  (let (
    (m (pkgShaPad s))
    (k pkgShaK)
    (h vecNew)
    (w vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
    (mut out "")
  )
    {
      (vecPush h 1779033703)
      (vecPush h 3144134277)
      (vecPush h 1013904242)
      (vecPush h 2773480762)
      (vecPush h 1359893119)
      (vecPush h 2600822924)
      (vecPush h 528734635)
      (vecPush h 1541459225)
      (while (< i 64)
        {
          (vecPush w 0)
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (set i 0)
      (while (< (* i 64) (vecLen m))
        {
          (pkgShaBlock m (* i 64) k h w)
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (set i 0)
      (while (< i 8)
        {
          (set out (concat out (fmtPadZerosLeft (fmtHex (vecGet h i)) 8)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      out
    }))

; Where a URL dependency lives: `.axiom/deps/<key>/` under the
; manifest's own directory (`pkgDepKey`: a readable slug and a hash of
; the whole URL), with a trailing slash like every other resolved
; dependency directory.
;
; The compiler never fetches. A manifest is a checked-in file that
; arrives with a clone, and a line in one may not run another
; project's network - the same rule that keeps a manifest `crate`
; from running cargo. A URL whose checkout is not there is refused
; with the path and the clone that fills it (see `pkgUrlHint`).
(pub :: pkgDepCheckout (-> String String String))
(pub fn (pkgDepCheckout base url)
  (concat (concat (pathWithSlash base) ".axiom/deps/") (concat (pkgDepKey url) "/")))

; The URL git recorded as `origin` for the checkout at `dir` (a
; directory path with its trailing slash), or "" when it records none.
;
; THIS IS WHAT A CHECKOUT IS, for `fetch` and for `build` alike: a
; directory whose `.git/config` says it was cloned from the URL the
; manifest names. A directory that merely EXISTS at the key's path is
; not one - until 2026-09-26 `fetch` answered `present` for any
; directory there, including the empty one its own failed clone had
; left behind (next fetch: exit 0, nothing checked out), and including
; one cloned from a different URL with the same slug. Reading git's own
; record rather than a file of ours means a checkout made by hand with
; the `git clone` the build's hint prints is as good as one `fetch`
; made, and one made from the wrong URL is not, whoever made it.
(pub :: pkgOrigin (-> String String))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgOrigin dir)
  (pkgOriginIn (readFile (concat dir ".git/config"))))

; The `url` of the `[remote "origin"]` section of git config text, or
; "": the first `url = VALUE` line after that section header and before
; the next one, whitespace trimmed and one pair of quotes removed.
(pub :: pkgOriginIn (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgOriginIn text)
  (let (
    (mut at 0)
    (mut inOrigin 0)
    (mut found "")
    (mut done 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (== done 0) (< at (strLen text)))
        (let (
          (eol (match (strFindByte text 10 at)
            ((None) (strLen text))
            ((Some k) k)))
          (line (strTrim (strSlice text at (- eol at))))
        )
          {
            (if (strStartsWith line "[")
              {
                (set inOrigin (if (strEq line "[remote \"origin\"]")
                  1
                  0))
                0
              }
              (if (&& (== inOrigin 1) (strStartsWith line "url"))
                (let ((rest (strTrim (strSlice line 3 (- (strLen line) 3)))))
                  (if (strStartsWith rest "=")
                    {
                      (set found (pkgUnquote (strTrim (strSlice rest 1 (- (strLen rest) 1)))))
                      (set done 1)
                      0
                    }
                    0))
                0))
            (set at (+ eol 1))
            0
          }))
      found
    }))

(pub :: pkgUnquote (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgUnquote v)
  (if (&& (>= (strLen v) 2) (&& (== (strByte v 0) 34) (== (strByte v (- (strLen v) 1)) 34)))
    (strSlice v 1 (- (strLen v) 2))
    v))

; Why the checkout at `dir` is not one of `url`, for a refusal: what git
; recorded instead, or that it recorded nothing.
(pub :: pkgOriginWhy (-> String String String))
(pub fn (pkgOriginWhy origin url)
  (if (== (strLen origin) 0)
    "it holds no git checkout with an `origin` (an interrupted or failed clone leaves one like this)"
    (concat "git records its origin as `" (concat origin "`"))))

; Every URL dependency's checkout was cloned from that URL - the build's
; half of what `pkgOrigin` says. Called after `pkgVerify` has refused a
; checkout that is not there at all, so each one here exists.
(pub :: pkgCheckOrigins (-> (Vec Int) String String Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgCheckOrigins urls base mf i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen urls))
    0
    (let (
      (url (vecGetStr urls i))
      (dest (pkgDepCheckout base (vecGetStr urls i)))
    )
      (let ((origin (pkgOrigin dest)))
        (if (strEq origin url)
          (pkgCheckOrigins urls base mf (+ i 1))
          (pkgDie (concat (concat "error: " (concat mf " depends on a registry URL whose checkout is not a clone of it:\n")) (concat (concat "       " (concat url "\n")) (concat (concat "       " (concat dest "\n")) (concat "       - " (concat (pkgOriginWhy origin url) ".\n       Remove that directory and run `axiom fetch`.\n")))))))))))

; Every registry (`depend` URL) value in an already-read manifest,
; in file order: what `fetch` checks out and nothing else.
(pub :: pkgFetchUrls (-> String (Vec Int)))
(pub fn (pkgFetchUrls text)
  (pkgFetchUrlsFrom (pkgValues text "depend") 0 vecNew))

(pub :: pkgFetchUrlsFrom (-> (Vec Int) Int (Vec Int) (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgFetchUrlsFrom vs i out)
  (if (>= i (vecLen vs))
    out
    {
      (let ((v (vecGetStr vs i)))
        (if (pkgIsUrl v)
          (vecPush
            out
            (cast Int v))
          (cast (Vec Int) 0)))
      (pkgFetchUrlsFrom vs (+ i 1) out)
    }))

; The hint a missing dependency directory carries when it is a URL
; dependency's checkout: the URL and the clone that fills it.
; Answers "" for a plain directory, so the refusal reads as before.
(pub :: pkgUrlHint (-> String String String String))
(pub fn (pkgUrlHint text base dir)
  (pkgUrlHintVals (pkgValues text "depend") base dir 0))

(pub :: pkgUrlHintVals (-> (Vec Int) String String Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgUrlHintVals vs base dir i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen vs))
    ""
    (let ((v (vecGetStr vs i)))
      (if (&& (pkgIsUrl v) (strEq (pkgDepCheckout base v) dir))
        (concat (concat "       `" (concat v "` is a registry dependency: check it out with `axiom fetch`,\n")) (concat "       or by hand with\n       git clone " (concat v (concat " " (concat dir "\n")))))
        (pkgUrlHintVals vs base dir (+ i 1))))))

; The `name` the manifest at or above `entryDir` declares, or "".
;
; This is what `axiom build` writes to when the command line says
; nothing. The driver reads it BEFORE `compileFile` and therefore
; before `pkgVerify` - a `let` binding is not a use - so the check that
; makes this safe is not the one that has already run; it is that
; `pkgVerify` exits 3 from inside `compileFile`, which is between this
; call and any write, and that `pkgNameOk` admits no `/` and no `..`
; anyway, so the string could not name another directory even if the
; verification were removed.
(pub :: pkgName (-> String String))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgName entryDir)
  (let ((mf (pkgFindManifest entryDir)))
    (if (== (strLen mf) 0)
      ""
      (pkgValue (readFile mf) "name"))))

; An `opt` value is one byte, `0` through `3`: the same four levels
; `--opt` honours, refused in the same words. Single byte, so no
; multi-digit value and no flag spelling can reach the emitter.
(pub :: pkgOptOk (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (pkgOptOk s)
  (if (== (strLen s) 1)
    (let ((c (strByte s 0)))
      (&& (>= c 48) (<= c 51)))
    false))

; The project's default optimisation level: the manifest's `opt`, or
; -1 when the manifest says nothing. The command line wins wherever
; it speaks; this is what `build`, `run` and `test` build at when it
; does not. Read AFTER `pkgVerify` wherever it is spent - an invalid
; `opt` exits 3 there, so the -1/level answer here is always honoured.
(pub :: pkgOpt (-> String Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgOpt entryDir)
  (let ((mf (pkgFindManifest entryDir)))
    (if (== (strLen mf) 0)
      (- 0 1)
      (let ((v (pkgValue (readFile mf) "opt")))
        (if (== (strLen v) 0)
          (- 0 1)
          (- (strByte v 0) 48))))))

; The project file for a manifest-mode invocation: the manifest's
; `main` resolved against the manifest's own directory, else
; `Main.ax` beside it, or "" when no manifest is found at all.
(pub :: pkgEntryFile (-> String String))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgEntryFile dir)
  (let ((mf (pkgFindManifest dir)))
    (if (== (strLen mf) 0)
      ""
      (let ((base (pathDir mf)))
        (let ((m (pkgValue (readFile mf) "main")))
          (if (> (strLen m) 0)
            (pkgResolve base m)
            (concat (pathWithSlash base) "Main.ax")))))))

; A package name is letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, and is not `.`
; or `..`. Deliberately narrower than the filesystem: the name is spent
; as a path, so `..` or a `/` in it would write the executable
; somewhere the command line never mentioned.
(pub :: pkgNameOk (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (pkgNameOk s)
  (if (== (strLen s) 0)
    false
    (if (|| (strEq s ".") (strEq s ".."))
      false
      (pkgNameBytesOk s 0))))

(pub :: pkgNameBytesOk (-> String Int Bool))
(pub fn (pkgNameBytesOk s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    true
    (if (pkgIsNameByte (strByte s i))
      (pkgNameBytesOk s (+ i 1))
      false)))

; `.` is 46, `-` is 45, `_` is 95.
(pub :: pkgIsNameByte (-> Int Bool))
(pub fn (pkgIsNameByte b)
  (|| (strIsAlpha b) (|| (strIsDigit b) (|| (== b 46) (|| (== b 45) (== b 95))))))

; `error: axiom.pkg:LINE: `, the prefix every line-level refusal here
; shares. The line number is 1-based and counts blank and comment lines
; like every editor does, because the reader's next move is to open the
; file at it.
(pub :: pkgAt (-> String Int String))
(pub fn (pkgAt mf line)
  (concat "error: " (concat mf (concat ":" (concat (fmtInt line) (concat ": " ""))))))

; Walk the manifest once and refuse every line that cannot mean what it
; says. Answers 0; every failure exits.
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS. Before it, the reader of a manifest was
; `pkgLineValue` and nothing else: a line it did not recognise
; contributed nothing and said nothing. Measured on 0.6.1, `dependd
; vendor/lib`, `depend` with no value, and `depend<TAB>vendor/lib` all
; produced the identical output - `error[AX5001]: cannot resolve import
; `Widget``, exit 1 - which names the import, names the directories
; that were searched, and never names the file that was supposed to
; have added one. That is this repository's most-hunted defect wearing
; a manifest: a configuration mistake reported as a source mistake,
; somewhere else, later.
;
; Four refusals, all of them the same shape - the file, the line, and
; what the line would have had to be:
;
;   1. A key that is not `name`, `version`, `depend`, `crate`,
;      `opt` or `main`.
;   2. A key with no value.
;   3. A second `name`, `version`, `opt` or `main`. The first wins in
;      `pkgValue`, so the second is a line the file contains and the
;      compiler ignores. `depend` and `crate` are exempt: repeating
;      either is how you declare two.
;   4. A `name` that is not a file name, since it is spent as one.
;   5. An `opt` that is not one of `0`, `1`, `2`, `3`.
(pub :: pkgCheckLines (-> String String Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgCheckLines text mf)
  (let (
    (lines (strSplit text 10))
    (n (vecLen lines))
    (mut i 0)
    (mut sawName 0)
    (mut sawVersion 0)
    (mut sawOpt 0)
    (mut sawMain 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (let ((t (pkgTrim (pkgUncomment (vecGetStr lines i)))))
            (if (== (strLen t) 0)
              0
              (let ((k (pkgKeyOf t)))
                (let ((v (pkgTrim (strSlice t (strLen k) (- (strLen t) (strLen k))))))
                  (if (pkgKnownKey k)
                    (if (== (strLen v) 0)
                      (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat (concat "`" (concat k "` has no value\n")) (concat "       `depend` takes a directory of modules and `crate` a crate\n" (concat "       directory; `name` and `version` take a word. The key and its value\n" "       are separated by any run of spaces or tabs.\n")))))
                      (if (strEq k "name")
                        {
                          (if (> sawName 0)
                            (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat "`name` is given twice\n" (concat "       The first would win and this one would be ignored, which is a\n" (concat "       manifest that says two things and means one.\n" "")))))
                            0)
                          (set sawName 1)
                          (if (pkgNameOk v)
                            0
                            (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat (concat "`name` is not a package name: `" (concat v "`\n")) (concat "       A package name is letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, and is\n" (concat "       neither `.` nor `..`. It is the file `axiom build` writes when\n" (concat "       no --output is given, so it names a FILE and not a path.\n" "")))))))
                          0
                        }
                        (if (strEq k "version")
                          {
                            (if (> sawVersion 0)
                              (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat "`version` is given twice\n" (concat "       The first would win and this one would be ignored, which is a\n" (concat "       manifest that says two things and means one.\n" "")))))
                              0)
                            (set sawVersion 1)
                            0
                          }
                          (if (strEq k "opt")
                            {
                              (if (> sawOpt 0)
                                (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat "`opt` is given twice\n" (concat "       The first would win and this one would be ignored, which is a\n" (concat "       manifest that says two things and means one.\n" "")))))
                                0)
                              (set sawOpt 1)
                              (if (pkgOptOk v)
                                0
                                (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat (concat "`opt` is not an optimisation level: `" (concat v "`\n")) (concat "       The manifest's `opt` is the project's default, honoured where\n" (concat "       `--opt` is silent; it answers one of `0`, `1`, `2` or `3`.\n" ""))))))
                              0
                            }
                            (if (strEq k "main")
                              {
                                (if (> sawMain 0)
                                  (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat "`main` is given twice\n" (concat "       The first would win and this one would be ignored, which is a\n" (concat "       manifest that says two things and means one.\n" "")))))
                                  0)
                                (set sawMain 1)
                                0
                              }
                              0)))))
                    (pkgDie (concat (pkgAt mf (+ i 1)) (concat (concat "unknown key `" (concat k "`\n")) (concat "       The manifest's keys are `name`, `version`, `depend`, `crate`, `opt`\n" (concat "       and `main`, and `#` starts a comment. An unknown key used to be ignored, which\n" (concat "       made a misspelled `depend` report as every module it would have\n" "       provided going missing - one at a time, naming this file never.\n")))))))))))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      0
    }))

; Refuse a manifest that cannot mean one thing.
;
; Every failure here is configuration rather than source, so all of
; them are reported the way the driver reports a bad command line - a
; plain `error:` line naming the file, and where there is one, the line
; - rather than as an `AXnnnn` with a span. There is no span: the fault
; is in a manifest, and the code ranges in `docs/diagnostics.md` are
; about what the SOURCE said.
;
;   0. Any line that cannot mean what it says: `pkgCheckLines`, which
;      runs FIRST, because a misspelled `depend` is not a dependency
;      whose directory is missing - it is a line nothing reads, and
;      reporting it as the former would name a path the file does not
;      contain.
;   1. A `depend` directory that is not a directory. Silence here means
;      every module it was meant to provide reports as missing, one at
;      a time, naming the module and never the manifest.
;   2. A `crate` directory that is not a directory, or holds no
;      `Cargo.toml`, or holds no `axiom/`. The first is `depend`'s
;      refusal again; the second is what makes `crate` MEAN crate
;      rather than be a second spelling of `depend`; the third is the
;      one a user actually hits, because `DIR/axiom/` is written by
;      `axiom-bindgen` and a fresh clone has not run it. All three are
;      refused rather than repaired: see the header.
;   3. Two dependencies providing a module of the same name - two
;      `depend`s, two crates, or one of each. Whichever came first
;      would win, and the other package's modules would compile
;      against declarations from a package they have never heard of.
;
; Answers 0, so a caller can bind it. Every failure exits.
(pub :: pkgVerify (-> String Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgVerify entryDir)
  (let ((mf (pkgFindManifest entryDir)))
    (if (== (strLen mf) 0)
      0
      (let ((text (readFile mf)))
        (let ((_lines (pkgCheckLines text mf)))
          (let ((dirs (pkgDependDirs entryDir)))
            (let (
              (n (vecLen dirs))
              (mut i 0)
            )
              {
                (while (< i n)
                  {
                    (if (isDir (vecGetStr dirs i))
                      0
                      (pkgDie (concat (concat "error: " (concat mf " names a dependency directory that is not there:\n")) (concat (concat "       " (concat (vecGetStr dirs i) "\n")) (concat "       `depend` names a DIRECTORY of modules, resolved against the\n" (concat "       manifest's own directory.\n" (pkgUrlHint text (pathDir mf) (vecGetStr dirs i))))))))
                    (set i (+ i 1))
                  })
                (pkgCheckOrigins (pkgFetchUrls text) (pathDir mf) mf 0)
                (pkgCheckCrates (pkgCrateDirs entryDir) mf 0)
                ; The pairwise check runs over the depend directories AND
                ; every crate's `axiom/` together, because they land in one
                ; search path and a clash between two of them is the same
                ; wrong answer whichever key declared them.
                (pkgCheckOverlap (pkgConcatDirs dirs (pkgCrateAxiomDirs entryDir)) mf 0)
              })))))))

; `a` then `b`, in order, in a NEW Vec - the caller is still holding
; both of them, and `pkgCrateAxiomDirs`' answer is also what
; `moduleSearchDirs` pushes.
(pub :: pkgConcatDirs (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Int) (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgConcatDirs a b)
  (let (
    (out vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen a))
        {
          (vecPush
            out
            (cast Int (vecGetStr a i)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (set i 0)
      (while (< i (vecLen b))
        {
          (vecPush
            out
            (cast Int (vecGetStr b i)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      out
    }))

; Every declared crate directory, refused for the three things it can
; fail to be. The messages name the DIRECTORY and what was looked for
; inside it, because "crate directory" is a shape the reader has to be
; told, not one they can infer from a path.
(pub :: pkgCheckCrates (-> (Vec Int) String Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgCheckCrates dirs mf i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen dirs))
    0
    {
      (let ((d (vecGetStr dirs i)))
        (if (isDir d)
          (if (fileExists (concat d "Cargo.toml"))
            (if (isDir (concat d "axiom/"))
              0
              (pkgDie (concat (concat "error: " (concat mf " names a crate with no generated module directory:\n")) (concat (concat "       " (concat (concat d "axiom/") "\n")) (concat "       A crate's binding module is written to `DIR/axiom/` by\n       `axiom-bindgen`, and a manifest `crate` does not run it: run\n" "       `axiom build --crate DIR` (or `axiom-bindgen`) once, or commit the\n       generated module, and this line is enough from then on.\n")))))
            (pkgDie (concat (concat "error: " (concat mf " names a crate directory with no Cargo.toml:\n")) (concat (concat "       " (concat (concat d "Cargo.toml") "\n")) (concat "       `crate` names a CARGO CRATE - a `Cargo.toml` beside an `axiom/`\n" "       holding its generated module. A plain directory of `.ax` modules\n       is `depend`, not `crate`.\n")))))
          (pkgDie (concat (concat "error: " (concat mf " names a crate directory that is not there:\n")) (concat (concat "       " (concat d "\n")) (concat "       `crate` names a DIRECTORY holding a `Cargo.toml` and an `axiom/`,\n" "       resolved against the manifest's own directory.\n"))))))
      (pkgCheckCrates dirs mf (+ i 1))
    }))

; Every pair of dependency directories, checked for a module both
; provide. Quadratic in the number of dependencies and linear in their
; contents, which is the right shape for a list a human typed.
(pub :: pkgCheckOverlap (-> (Vec Int) String Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgCheckOverlap dirs mf i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen dirs))
    0
    {
      (pkgCheckPair dirs mf i (+ i 1))
      (pkgCheckOverlap dirs mf (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: pkgCheckPair (-> (Vec Int) String Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgCheckPair dirs mf i j)
  (if (>= j (vecLen dirs))
    0
    {
      (let ((clash (pkgFirstShared (vecGetStr dirs i) (vecGetStr dirs j))))
        (if (== (strLen clash) 0)
          0
          (pkgDie (concat (concat "error: two dependencies in " (concat mf " provide the module `")) (concat (concat (pkgModuleName clash) (concat "`:\n" "")) (concat (concat "       " (concat (concat (vecGetStr dirs i) clash) "\n")) (concat "       " (concat (concat (vecGetStr dirs j) clash) (concat "\n" "       One of them would win by declaration order and the other's\n       modules would compile against declarations they never named.\n")))))))))
      (pkgCheckPair dirs mf i (+ j 1))
    }))

; The first module file both directories hold, as a path relative to
; each, or "".
;
; THIS USED TO BE ONE `sysReadDir`. `depend a` and `depend b` each
; holding `Sub/Widget.ax` - what `(import Sub.Widget)` resolves to -
; passed this check and the program exited with `a`'s answer, silently,
; which is the exact failure the whole file exists to refuse, one
; directory down. `pkgModules` descends.
(pub :: pkgFirstShared (-> String String String))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgFirstShared a b)
  (let ((mods (pkgModules a)))
    (let (
      (mut i 0)
      (mut hit "")
    )
      {
        (while (< i (vecLen mods))
          {
            (let ((name (vecGetStr mods i)))
              (if (&& (== (strLen hit) 0) (fileExists (concat b name)))
                {
                  (set hit name)
                  0
                }
                0))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        hit
      })))

; Every `.ax` file under `dir`, as a path relative to it - `Widget.ax`,
; `Sub/Widget.ax`.
;
; THREE LEVELS DEEP, stated rather than derived, the way
; `pkgFindManifestFrom` states its eight. It is the depth the LSP's
; import resolver is already documented at (`docs/lsp.md`), and a
; module nested deeper than `A.B.C` is one nobody in this repository
; writes. The walk runs once inside `pkgVerify`, per declared
; dependency, and never per module lookup.
(pub :: pkgModules (-> String (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgModules dir)
  (let ((out vecNew))
    {
      (pkgWalkModules out dir "" 0)
      out
    }))

(pub :: pkgWalkModules (-> (Vec Int) String String Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgWalkModules out root rel depth)
  (if (> depth 2)
    0
    (pkgWalkEntries
      out
      root
      rel
      (sysReadDir (strCStr (concat root rel)))
      0
      depth)))

(pub :: pkgWalkEntries (-> (Vec Int) String String (Vec String) Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (pkgWalkEntries out root rel entries i depth)
  (if (>= i (vecLen entries))
    0
    {
      ; `sysReadDir` answers `.` and `..` with everything else - it is
      ; the raw call's answer on purpose, so an empty Vec can mean
      ; failure and nothing else - and descending into either is how a
      ; three-level bound becomes an infinite walk.
      (let ((name (vecGetStr entries i)))
        (if (|| (strEq name ".") (strEq name ".."))
          0
          (let ((r (concat rel name)))
            (if (isDir (concat root r))
              (pkgWalkModules out root (concat r "/") (+ depth 1))
              (if (strEq (pathExt name) ".ax")
                {
                  (vecPush
                    out
                    (cast Int r))
                  0
                }
                0)))))
      (pkgWalkEntries
        out
        root
        rel
        entries
        (+ i 1)
        depth)
    }))

; `Sub/Widget.ax` -> `Sub.Widget`: the name an `import` spells, which
; is what a reader has to type to reproduce the clash. `pathStem` was
; here before nesting existed and answered `Widget` for both, so the
; message named a module no `import` in the project mentions.
(pub :: pkgModuleName (-> String String))
(pub fn (pkgModuleName rel)
  (pkgDotSlashes
    (if (strEq (pathExt rel) ".ax")
      (strSlice rel 0 (- (strLen rel) 3))
      rel)
    0))

(pub :: pkgDotSlashes (-> String Int String))
(pub fn (pkgDotSlashes s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    s
    (if (== (strByte s i) 47)
      (pkgDotSlashes (concat (strSlice s 0 i) (concat "." (strSlice s (+ i 1) (- (strLen s) (+ i 1))))) (+ i 1))
      (pkgDotSlashes s (+ i 1)))))

; The driver's refusal shape: the message on fd 2, exit 3 - this
; compiler's code for "a module could not be read or parsed", which is
; what a manifest naming a module twice or naming nothing amounts to.
(pub :: pkgDie (-> String Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (pkgDie msg)
  {
    (writeStr 2 msg)
    (sysExitWith 3)
    0
  })
